WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.777
Phone validation becomes easier after normalization.

00:00:03.777 --> 00:00:08.492
The query first removes punctuation and other non-digit characters.

00:00:08.492 --> 00:00:11.902
Then it checks the length of the resulting digit string.

00:00:11.902 --> 00:00:17.552
If there are exactly ten digits, the phone receives a valid-shape status.

00:00:17.552 --> 00:00:22.507
If the original value is missing or blank, it is labeled missing.

00:00:22.507 --> 00:00:24.748
Other cases are sent for review.

00:00:25.291 --> 00:00:30.413
Imagine two phone values. One uses parentheses and a dash.

00:00:30.413 --> 00:00:34.688
The other contains exactly the same number with no punctuation.

00:00:34.688 --> 00:00:37.998
If we compare the raw strings, they look different.

00:00:37.998 --> 00:00:41.288
After digit extraction, they become identical.

00:00:41.288 --> 00:00:46.393
That is why normalization should often happen before validation or matching.

00:00:46.393 --> 00:00:53.709
When you run the query, inspect the original phone, the digits-only value, and the final status together.
